    {"step": 1, "tactic": "Reconnaissance / Gather Victim Host Information — Client Configurations", "technique": "T1592.004", "what_happened": "At 2027-08-14 09:11:05 CEST, Suricata logged a burst of HTTP GET requests from data.srcip=45.83.142.61 to data.dstip=10.108.57.211 walking a fixed list of public-facing discovery files — /robots.txt, /sitemap.xml, /sitemap_index.xml, /humans.txt, /security.txt, /.well-known/security.txt, /.well-known/openid-configuration, /.well-known/host-meta, /.well-known/dnt-policy.txt, /favicon.ico, /manifest.json. Each request used a single fixed Chrome 120 User-Agent and was spaced ~25 seconds apart (polite timing). Suricata fired 'ET INFO HTTP Headers Used By Discovery Tools' on the sequence. This is the passive web-discovery opening move of client-configuration profiling — the operator harvests every machine-readable endpoint description file the host exposes before touching the application itself.", "chain_reason": "Suricata signature 'ET INFO HTTP Headers Used By Discovery Tools' with data.srcip=45.83.142.61 against data.dstip=10.108.57.211, data.url iterating across a fixed discovery-file list within a 5-minute window from a single source.", "first_seen": "2027-08-14T09:11:05+0200"},
    {"step": 2, "tactic": "Reconnaissance / Gather Victim Host Information — Client Configurations", "technique": "T1592.004", "what_happened": "At 09:19:40 CEST (~3-minute deliberate pause after the discovery-file walk ended), the same data.srcip=45.83.142.61 began a multi-User-Agent probing run against data.url=/ on 10.108.57.211 — cycling 11 distinct data.http.user_agent strings spanning desktop browsers (Chrome 120, Edge 119, Firefox 121, Safari 16.6), mobile (iPhone iOS 17, Android 13), SDK clients (python-requests/2.31.0, curl/8.0.1, Apache-HttpClient/4.5.13), a Googlebot impersonation string and a legacy Trident/7.0 IE11 string. Each probe was spaced ~40 seconds apart. Suricata fired 'ET POLICY Outdated Browser User-Agent' when the legacy IE11 and SDK User-Agents were observed. The goal is to see whether the server returns different content, redirects or status codes per UA family — a fingerprint of the client mix the host expects.", "chain_reason": "Suricata signature 'ET POLICY Outdated Browser User-Agent' and repeated GET requests with data.srcip=45.83.142.61, data.dstip=10.108.57.211, data.url=/ and a rotating data.http.user_agent value across 11 distinct strings within an 8-minute window — single source, single target, rotating client identity.", "first_seen": "2027-08-14T09:19:40+0200"},
    {"step": 3, "tactic": "Reconnaissance / Gather Victim Host Information — Client Configurations", "technique": "T1592.004", "what_happened": "At 09:28:55 CEST, the same data.srcip=45.83.142.61 opened a sequence of TLS connections to 10.108.57.211:443 with deliberately varied Client Hello parameters — four cipher-suite profiles (a modern HIGH suite set, a legacy AES128-SHA/DES-CBC3-SHA set, a TLS1.2-only HIGH set, and a permissive ALL set) plus SNI set to the target IP. Suricata's TLS event logging captured the connections and emitted JA3 hash entries for each distinct Client Hello. This is JA3/JA4 client-fingerprint baselining: the operator records exactly how the server negotiates against each client TLS stack so a future phishing payload can mimic an accepted fingerprint.", "chain_reason": "Suricata TLS events with data.srcip=45.83.142.61, data.dstip=10.108.57.211, data.dstport=443 and multiple distinct JA3 hashes recorded within a 6-minute window, correlated to the same scanning source from steps 1-2.", "first_seen": "2027-08-14T09:28:55+0200"},
    {"step": 4, "tactic": "Reconnaissance / Gather Victim Host Information — Client Configurations", "technique": "T1592.004", "what_happened": "At 09:34:10 CEST, the same data.srcip=45.83.142.61 issued HTTP/2 negotiation probes against 10.108.57.211 — an HTTP/2-over-TLS HEAD request advertising ALPN, followed by an HTTP/2 prior-knowledge cleartext upgrade attempt on port 80. Suricata logged the ALPN negotiation and protocol-upgrade attempts. This closes the client-configuration profile: the operator now knows the host's supported HTTP versions, ALPN tokens, TLS stack behaviour, UA-conditional content and exposed metadata files. The cumulative ~25-minute polite-timing run with multiple inter-step pauses is the canonical T1592.004 client-configuration profiling fingerprint.", "chain_reason": "Suricata correlation: ALPN/HTTP2 negotiation events with data.srcip=45.83.142.61, data.dstip=10.108.57.211 on data.dstport in {80,443}, immediately following steps 1-3 from the same source within the same 25-minute window — rule.groups containing 'suricata' + 'recon'.", "first_seen": "2027-08-14T09:34:10+0200"}
